The best product work starts when the common infrastructure stops demanding attention. Authentication, billing, storage, email, and security are essential, but they are rarely what makes a product unique.

## Keep the Architecture Direct

This application uses a small set of layers with clear responsibilities:

- Handlers translate HTTP requests and responses
- Services own business rules
- Repositories own SQL and persistence
- templ components render the interface

The separation makes product changes easier to understand without hiding behavior behind a framework.

## Treat Providers as Replaceable

Payments and storage sit behind narrow interfaces because they are real integration boundaries. Product services can rely on capabilities instead of vendor SDKs.

## Keep Security in the Foundation

CSRF protection, CSP nonces, security headers, token expiry, webhook signature validation, and a narrow render configuration with no secrets should exist before customer data arrives.

## Build the Product Layer Next

Once the foundation is stable, add the workflows that customers actually pay for. The existing structure is there to support those features, not constrain them.
